  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Periods of light to moderate rain tonight\nthrough Wednesday. Greatest areal coverage tonight and Tuesday \nmorning. Widespread flooding unlikely, isolated minor flooding \npossible.\n\nA quasi-stnry area of mid-level frontogenesis beneath the right\nentrance region of upper jet max will strengthen into the \nmiddle of the overnight hours over the I-80 corridor in West- \nCentral PA (Near KFIG to KUNV) before moving gradually south and\nweakening on Tuesday as the best lift associated with the \naforementioned jet quadrant shifts to a location SE of Cape Cod\nwhile a second jetlet focused some meso-B UVVEL across the \nLaurel Highlands and points south. \n\nRainfall rates thus far per MRMS have maxed out between 0.10 and\n0.25 of an inch per hour, but part of these higher rates area\nlikely the result of bright-banding around the 9 kft AGL (from \neach RDA site such as KCLE, KPIT, KCCX and KBUF). \n\nThese rainfall rates (at least for the time being) are well \nbelow the amounts needed to pose a flooding threat. \n\nLocations that will see between 0.50 and 1.00 inch overnight\nwill be near or just SW of the current main area of the rain. \n \nThe Laurel Highlands, mainly our SW 4 counties, have the lowest\nFFG/FFHs. But, much of the statistical guidance keeps rain \nintensities (QPF/1,3&6hr time frames) above those thresholds for\nflooding to occur. There is still a ~20% chc for rainfall \nintensities to exceed FFG tonight/Tues AM in the SW, but we'd \nneed to get some instability and deep convection to tap all the \n1.5\" PWAT overhead. We're going to continue to hold off on any \nflood watches. \n\nExpect some minor (ponding of water on roads/ditches, and small\ncreeks/streams) flooding due to the extended duration of the \nlight to moderate rainfall. Dry air continues to be shoved into \nthe area from the N through the next 48 hours. A tight gradient \nof precip may occur along or just north of I-80 this evening as \na slight enhancement to the rain is expected while a short wave \ntrough/MCV moves overhead W to E along I-80. There could be a \nltg strike/thunder rumble here or there, but it will be sparse.\n\nWe'll continue to evaluate the near term guidance for hints of\nheavier than currently-expected precip.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Trending drier later in the week as a coastal \nstorm develops offshore.\n\nNortheast flow Wednesday into Thursday between 1038 mb high in\nQuebec and a developing coastal low will advect in dewpoints in\nthe 40s and perhaps even the 30s at the sfc Wed-Thu, with even\ndrier air in the 500-850 mb layer. This should bring an end to\nthe rainfall for at least a couple of days. The next feature to\nmonitor will be the coastal storm, which may meander off the New\nEngland and Mid Atlantic coast into the early part of next week\nwith this blocking high to its north. Such blocky patterns \nare notoriously difficult to predict with respect to sfc\nfeatures and precip potential/placement/timing. Depending on the\nsize of the coastal storm and how much it retrogrades, rain will\nbe possible in at least east central PA between Fri and early\nnext week, but confidence is low. Higher confidence in wind \ngusts of 15-25 mph and below normal temps through the end of the\nweek.",
